Today has been one of the most fulfilling days of my life. Three weeks ago, I found a tiny golden puppy shivering near our society gate with a badly injured paw. Seeing his terrified whimper, I couldn''t walk away. With Dr. Verma''s medical help, we cleaned the wound, administered antibiotics, and nurtured him back to health on our porch. Today, our compassionate neighbor, Mrs. Sen, officially adopted him and named him ''Sheru''. Watching Sheru wag his tail joyfully and nuzzle into his new warm bed brought tears to my eyes. Small acts of kindness possess enormous power to heal broken lives. I go to sleep with a heart brimming with peace.
Goodnight,
Anmol', '2 Marks for format (Day, Date, Salutation) and voice; 2 Marks for narrative coherence and emotional depth; 1 Mark for mechanics', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90040017-0000-4000-8000-00000000000e', 'Q14. You recently visited an old age home with your school voluntary service club. Write a Descriptive Paragraph in about 100-120 words describing your observations, the residents'' emotions, and the impact the visit left on your mind.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'English', 'Descriptive Paragraph', 5, 'A Visit to the Old Age Home: Last Saturday, our school voluntary club visited ''Sandhya Aashray'', a serene refuge for senior citizens. Walking into the sunny courtyard, we were greeted by silver-haired elders whose eyes reflected both wisdom and an unspoken yearning for companionship. We spent the afternoon singing melodious vintage songs, reciting poetry, and listening to their poignant life journeys. Mrs. Shanti, an eighty-year-old retired teacher, affectionately held my hand while reminiscing about her teaching days. Despite physical frailties, their grace and warm smiles were deeply touching. However, a lingering melancholy pervaded the corridors, revealing the quiet sting of abandonment by their busy families. The experience awakened in me a profound sense of gratitude and reaffirmed that our elders deserve dignity, unconditional love, and constant care.', '2 Marks for content relevance and vivid sensory descriptions; 2 Marks for organization and vocabulary; 1 Mark for grammatical accuracy', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90040017-0000-4000-8000-00000000000f', 'Q15. Short Answer (Moments): In ''The Lost Child'', describe the transformation in the little child''s desires before and after he got separated from his parents at the spring fair.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'English', 'The Lost Child', 5, 'Before getting lost, the child was dazzled by the vibrant fair, longing for toys, dragonflies, burfi sweets, colorful balloons, and a ride on the roundabout. However, the moment he realized he was separated, overwhelming panic gripped him. All worldly attractions lost their allure completely; he sobbed uncontrollably, rejecting all gifts and crying only for his father and mother, showing that parental presence transcends all material desires.', '1.5 Marks for contrasting material longing with separation panic; 1.5 Marks for parent supremacy conclusion', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90040017-0000-4000-8000-000000000010', 'Q16. Long Answer (Beehive / Moments): In ''The Last Leaf'', Behrman sacrificed his life to save young Johnsy. Elucidate how Behrman''s masterpiece was not a painting on a gallery canvas, but an act of supreme selfless love that rekindled the will to live.', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'English', 'The Last Leaf', 5, 'In O. Henry''s ''The Last Leaf'', young artist Johnsy fell severely ill with pneumonia and became psychologically convinced that she would die when the last ivy leaf fell from the brick wall outside her window. Old Behrman, a sixty-year-old struggling artist whose lifelong dream was to paint a masterpiece, learned of her despair. On a freezing, stormy night with torrential rain and sleet, Behrman climbed a ladder with a lantern and painted a single, lifelike ivy leaf on the wall after the real leaf fell. Seeing the leaf withstand the howling tempest, Johnsy realized the sin of wishing to die, and her will to live was restored. Behrman contracted pneumonia from the freezing rain and died shortly after. His leaf was a true masterpiece—not of commercial art, but of supreme self-sacrifice and compassion that saved a dying human life.', '2 Marks for Johnsy''s psychological despair; 2 Marks for Behrman''s nocturnal painting in storm; 2 Marks for evaluating the masterpiece as supreme human sacrifice', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
