Today has been one of the most fulfilling days of my life. Three weeks ago, I found a tiny golden puppy shivering near our society gate with a badly injured paw. Seeing his terrified whimper, I couldn''t walk away. With Dr. Verma''s medical help, we cleaned the wound, administered antibiotics, and nurtured him back to health on our porch. Today, our compassionate neighbor, Mrs. Sen, officially adopted him and named him ''Sheru''. Watching Sheru wag his tail joyfully and nuzzle into his new warm bed brought tears to my eyes. Small acts of kindness possess enormous power to heal broken lives. I go to sleep with a heart brimming with peace.
Goodnight,
Anmol', '2 Marks for format (Day, Date, Salutation) and voice; 2 Marks for narrative coherence and emotional depth; 1 Mark for mechanics', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90040023-0000-4000-8000-00000000000e', 'Q14. You recently visited an old age home with your school voluntary service club. Write a Descriptive Paragraph in about 100-120 words describing your observations, the residents'' emotions, and the impact the visit left on your mind.', NULL, NULL, 'LA', 'English', 'Descriptive Paragraph', 5, 'A Visit to the Old Age Home: Last Saturday, our school voluntary club visited ''Sandhya Aashray'', a serene refuge for senior citizens. Walking into the sunny courtyard, we were greeted by silver-haired elders whose eyes reflected both wisdom and an unspoken yearning for companionship. We spent the afternoon singing melodious vintage songs, reciting poetry, and listening to their poignant life journeys. Mrs. Shanti, an eighty-year-old retired teacher, affectionately held my hand while reminiscing about her teaching days. Despite physical frailties, their grace and warm smiles were deeply touching. However, a lingering melancholy pervaded the corridors, revealing the quiet sting of abandonment by their busy families. The experience awakened in me a profound sense of gratitude and reaffirmed that our elders deserve dignity, unconditional love, and constant care.', '2 Marks for content relevance and vivid sensory descriptions; 2 Marks for organization and vocabulary; 1 Mark for grammatical accuracy', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90040023-0000-4000-8000-00000000000f', 'Q15. Reference to Context (Beehive Prose): ''Margie was scornful. "School? What''s there to write about school? I hate school." Margie always hated school, but now she hated it more than ever.'' (i) Why did Margie hate her mechanical school? (ii) What had the County Inspector done to fix her mechanical teacher? (iii) What kind of school did Tommy describe from the old printed book?', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'English', 'The Fun They Had', 5, '(i) Margie hated her mechanical school because a cold computerized machine gave her continuous geography tests where her performance kept declining, isolating her in an emotionless bedroom classroom. (ii) The County Inspector dismantled the machine and slowed down its geography sector to an average ten-year-old cognitive level. (iii) Tommy described an ancient school where all human children from the neighborhood gathered in a shared building, learning the same lessons together under human teachers.', '1.5 Marks for Margie''s robotic isolation; 1.5 Marks for Inspector''s recalibration; 2 Marks for description of human historical schools', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90040023-0000-4000-8000-000000000010', 'Q16. Reference to Context (Poetry - The Road Not Taken): ''Two roads diverged in a yellow wood, / And sorry I could not travel both / And be one traveler, long I stood / And looked down one as far as I could...'' (i) What do the ''two diverging roads'' symbolize in human life? (ii) Why did the poet choose the second road? (iii) What does the phrase ''yellow wood'' signify?', NULL, NULL, 'CASE', 'English', 'The Road Not Taken', 5, '(i) The two diverging roads symbolize the inevitable, fateful choices and decisions that every individual must make in the journey of life. (ii) The poet chose the second road because it was grassy, less traveled by the crowd, and ''wanted wear'', representing an unconventional, bold path. (iii) ''Yellow wood'' signifies the autumn season, representing maturity, change, and the passage of time.', '1.5 Marks for symbolical interpretation of roads; 2 Marks for justifying road choice; 1.5 Marks for autumn season significance', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90040023-0000-4000-8000-000000000011', 'Q17. (Beehive) Short Answer (Beehive): How did Evelyn Glennie achieve her monumental musical success despite losing her hearing ability at a young age? What inspiration does she offer to differently-abled people?', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'English', 'The Sound of Music', 3, 'Evelyn Glennie refused to surrender to profound deafness. Guided by percussionist Ron Forbes, she learned to sense acoustic vibrations through different parts of her body rather than her ears—feeling higher drums from the waist up and lower drums from the waist down. Through iron willpower and unremitting practice, she mastered over a thousand percussion instruments and became a celebrated international soloist, proving that physical impairments cannot constrain determination.', '1.5 Marks for Ron Forbes guidance and vibration sensing technique; 1.5 Marks for inspirational message of perseverance', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
        ('90040023-0000-4000-8000-000000000012', 'Q18. (Beehive) Short Answer (Moments): In ''The Lost Child'', describe the transformation in the little child''s desires before and after he got separated from his parents at the spring fair.', NULL, NULL, 'SA', 'English', 'The Lost Child', 3, 'Before getting lost, the child was dazzled by the vibrant fair, longing for toys, dragonflies, burfi sweets, colorful balloons, and a ride on the roundabout. However, the moment he realized he was separated, overwhelming panic gripped him. All worldly attractions lost their allure completely; he sobbed uncontrollably, rejecting all gifts and crying only for his father and mother, showing that parental presence transcends all material desires.', '1.5 Marks for contrasting material longing with separation panic; 1.5 Marks for parent supremacy conclusion', NULL, NULL, 'MEDIUM', true),
